WEBVTT

00:19.728 --> 00:20.812
[door beeps]

00:20.937 --> 00:22.063
[woman] Thanks.

00:36.786 --> 00:38.121
[indistinct chatter]

00:39.539 --> 00:41.207
[breathing heavily]

00:43.334 --> 00:44.919
[indistinct chatter]

01:01.102 --> 01:02.145
[sighs]

01:11.780 --> 01:12.947
[doorknob rattling]

01:13.031 --> 01:14.741
- No.
- [bangs door]

01:14.824 --> 01:16.034
[sighs]

01:18.703 --> 01:20.830
- [gun cocking]
- Hands up. Hands up!

01:20.914 --> 01:23.625
Whoa, whoa, whoa,
whoa, whoa, whoa.

01:23.708 --> 01:25.502
Forgot all the cameras, bub?

01:25.585 --> 01:28.463
Look, Dr. Brenner asked
for me specifically. Okay?

01:28.546 --> 01:29.672
How else do you think
I got in here?

01:29.756 --> 01:30.799
[Hopper scoffs]

01:31.466 --> 01:32.592
What's your name again?

01:32.675 --> 01:34.385
It's Jim Hopper.

01:34.469 --> 01:36.179
Chief Jim Hopper.

01:36.262 --> 01:38.139
Yeah, I've got Jim Hopper--
[grunts]

01:39.599 --> 01:41.184
- [man on radio] Come again?
- [grunts]

01:42.227 --> 01:43.728
[both panting]

01:43.812 --> 01:45.772
[indistinct radio chatter]

01:45.855 --> 01:47.440
Hey...

01:47.524 --> 01:49.025
you mind if I borrow this one?

01:49.567 --> 01:51.444
- [beeps]
- [door buzzing]

01:55.240 --> 01:56.407
[door locks]

02:00.036 --> 02:02.413
- [liquid pouring]
- [Lonnie] Drink.

02:02.497 --> 02:03.790
It'll calm your nerves.

02:05.250 --> 02:07.127
Help you think straight, yeah?

02:11.881 --> 02:13.758
I don't know what to do.

02:15.135 --> 02:16.594
I know. I know.

02:18.012 --> 02:20.265
[sniffs] This whole time...

02:22.058 --> 02:25.603
I... I could... I could feel him.

02:25.687 --> 02:26.980
He was...

02:27.897 --> 02:30.066
He was so close.
He was... he was right there.

02:30.150 --> 02:32.902
- I knew he was alive.
- [thunder rumbles]

02:32.986 --> 02:37.782
Our hands...
our hands were almost touching.

02:37.866 --> 02:40.869
Now it's like I... uh...

02:42.412 --> 02:45.165
[sobbing] God, it's like
I can't feel him anymore.

02:46.916 --> 02:49.294
- Don't look at me like that.
- Like what?

02:49.377 --> 02:51.421
Like how everybody is looking at me.

02:51.504 --> 02:53.423
- Like I'm out of my damn mind.
- Hey.

02:56.342 --> 02:57.385
You're not gonna like this,

02:57.468 --> 03:01.097
but I think you need to seriously consider
the possibility that all this...

03:03.433 --> 03:04.475
It's in your head.

03:04.559 --> 03:05.852
[scoffs]

03:06.978 --> 03:08.980
- You remember your Aunt Darlene?
- No.

03:09.063 --> 03:12.025
No, this is not that.

03:12.108 --> 03:13.318
I mean, when something like this happens,

03:13.401 --> 03:16.446
your mind makes up stuff
for you to cope, you know?

03:17.697 --> 03:19.949
I mean, Jesus, there's a funeral
tomorrow for our little boy

03:20.033 --> 03:21.951
and you're saying his body is fake.

03:23.202 --> 03:24.495
He's in the wall.

03:25.204 --> 03:27.165
I mean, how do you explain that?

03:29.042 --> 03:31.586
- [stammers]
- It just doesn't make sense. It doesn't.

03:31.669 --> 03:35.381
- At least go talk to a shrink or...
- [sighs]

03:35.465 --> 03:38.509
- What about Pastor Charles or someone--
- I don't-- Well, they can't help.

03:38.593 --> 03:40.470
Joyce, you just told me...

03:41.387 --> 03:42.805
that Will is gone.

03:44.891 --> 03:47.852
- What else is there to do?
- [sniffles]

03:51.231 --> 03:52.398
Hey.

03:59.489 --> 04:00.531
[sniffles]

04:06.371 --> 04:08.164
[Hopper] Will?

04:09.457 --> 04:10.625
Will?

04:12.919 --> 04:14.295
Will?

04:16.297 --> 04:17.465
Will?

04:37.902 --> 04:40.280
[Mike] What was Will saying?

04:40.363 --> 04:42.573
Like home... Like home...

04:43.992 --> 04:45.451
but dark?

04:46.452 --> 04:47.787
[Lucas] And empty.

04:49.038 --> 04:53.334
[sighs] Empty and cold.
Wait, did he say cold?

04:53.418 --> 04:56.504
I don't know.
The stupid radio kept going in and out.

04:56.587 --> 04:58.006
[sighs] It's like riddles in the dark.

04:58.089 --> 04:59.966
Like home.
Like his house?

05:00.049 --> 05:01.301
Or maybe like Hawkins.

05:01.384 --> 05:02.927
Upside down.

05:03.011 --> 05:05.263
- What'd she say?
- Upside down.

05:05.346 --> 05:06.889
- [Lucas] What?
- Upside down.

05:07.890 --> 05:11.769
When El showed us where Will was,
she flipped the board over, remember?

05:11.853 --> 05:13.813
Upside down.

05:13.896 --> 05:15.523
Dark. Empty.

05:15.606 --> 05:17.942
Do you understand what he's talking about?

05:18.026 --> 05:19.944
- No.
- [Mike] Guys, come on, think about it.

05:20.028 --> 05:23.072
When El took us to find Will,
she took us to his house, right?

05:23.156 --> 05:24.949
Yeah.
And he wasn't there.

05:25.033 --> 05:26.743
But what if he was there?

05:26.826 --> 05:28.786
What if we just couldn't see him?

05:28.870 --> 05:30.830
What if he was on the other side?

05:32.040 --> 05:35.084
What if this is Hawkins and...

05:36.544 --> 05:37.754
this is where Will is?

05:37.837 --> 05:39.464
The Upside Down.

05:39.547 --> 05:41.257
Like the Vale of Shadows.

05:43.217 --> 05:44.635
[Hopper] Will?

05:45.928 --> 05:48.222
- Will?
- [alarm blaring faintly]

05:51.434 --> 05:52.602
Will?

05:54.187 --> 05:55.980
[alarm continues]

06:00.485 --> 06:02.445
- [pressing button]
- [indistinct chatter]

06:03.696 --> 06:05.364
[guards yelling]

06:08.242 --> 06:10.286
I see him! Hey! Stop!

06:16.751 --> 06:18.252
[Dustin] "The Vale of Shadows

06:18.336 --> 06:22.590
is a dimension that is a dark reflection
or echo of our world.

06:28.346 --> 06:31.641
It is a place of decay and death."

06:31.724 --> 06:33.059
[Hopper] Will?

06:33.142 --> 06:37.021
"A plane out of phase.
A place of monsters.

06:39.440 --> 06:41.859
It is right next to you,
and you don't even see it."

06:41.943 --> 06:44.320
- [electricity crackling]
- [Hopper coughing] Will?

06:46.155 --> 06:47.490
Will?

06:47.573 --> 06:49.575
An alternate dimension.

06:50.576 --> 06:52.495
But... how... how do we get there?

06:52.578 --> 06:53.621
You cast Shadow Walk.

06:53.704 --> 06:54.997
In real life, dummy.

06:55.081 --> 06:57.083
We can't shadow walk, but...

06:58.042 --> 06:59.460
maybe she can.

07:01.254 --> 07:03.923
Do you know how we get there?
To the Upside Down?

07:07.009 --> 07:08.678
[sighing] Oh, my God!

07:15.435 --> 07:17.311
[coughing]

07:19.730 --> 07:21.357
[exclaims softly]

07:23.151 --> 07:24.569
What the hell?

07:25.903 --> 07:27.572
[rhythmic pulsing]

07:51.637 --> 07:52.680
[gasps]

08:08.154 --> 08:09.197
[Hopper] Hey!

08:09.989 --> 08:11.282
Hey! Hey!

08:15.786 --> 08:17.788
[theme music playing]

09:28.901 --> 09:30.528
Hey, kid.

09:30.611 --> 09:32.405
What's going on?

09:32.488 --> 09:36.576
Your dad's, uh, gonna stay here tonight.
[stammers] On the couch.

09:36.659 --> 09:38.703
[Lonnie] Yeah, I'm here
as long as you need me, okay?

09:38.786 --> 09:40.580
How are you holding up?

09:44.166 --> 09:45.334
What happened?

09:45.418 --> 09:47.044
- Don't worry about that.
- Mom...

09:47.795 --> 09:50.756
that thing you saw before,
did it come back?

09:50.840 --> 09:53.551
Jonathan, that's enough.

09:53.634 --> 09:55.344
[sighs]

09:55.428 --> 09:57.054
Can we talk?

09:57.138 --> 09:58.306
Alone?

09:59.140 --> 10:01.350
- You need to leave.
- Look, I know you're upset.

10:01.434 --> 10:03.019
We all are.

10:03.102 --> 10:07.189
But you need to listen to me.
Your mother is sick. Really sick.

10:07.273 --> 10:08.774
Yeah. Well, you being here,

10:08.858 --> 10:10.067
you're just making things worse,
like always.

10:10.151 --> 10:11.193
- Worse?
- Yeah!

10:11.277 --> 10:13.237
She took down that wall with an ax.

10:13.321 --> 10:15.406
She said that Will was inside
and that he's talking to her.

10:15.489 --> 10:16.991
Yeah.
Maybe he was.

10:17.074 --> 10:18.826
This isn't some kind of joke.

10:18.909 --> 10:20.911
Your mom was half frozen to death
when I got here.

10:20.995 --> 10:22.371
Trembling, scared out of her mind.

10:22.455 --> 10:24.707
You come in here and you start
feeding into her hallucinations

10:24.790 --> 10:25.958
or whatever the hell you want to call it,

10:26.042 --> 10:27.543
you're gonna push her right over the edge.

10:28.711 --> 10:30.004
You hear me?

10:32.298 --> 10:35.384
[sighs] Look, I'm on your side.

10:35.468 --> 10:36.761
I'm here to help.

10:37.678 --> 10:39.639
I'm gonna make things better
around here for all of us.

10:39.722 --> 10:42.433
[scoffs] Thank God you're here.

10:44.060 --> 10:46.687
Do me a favor.
At the funeral tomorrow, just behave.

10:47.730 --> 10:49.482
If not for me, for your mother.

10:53.486 --> 10:56.947
Take that down.
It's inappropriate.

11:05.373 --> 11:07.833
- Good.
- Ah! Dad, you're... you're choking me.

11:07.917 --> 11:10.628
Ah... there.
It's supposed to be a little tight.

11:11.629 --> 11:12.797
Looks good.

11:12.880 --> 11:15.257
- [zipper closing]
- Here we go. [sighs]

11:16.717 --> 11:18.344
You look nice, sweetie.

11:21.222 --> 11:22.681
Anything else?

11:22.765 --> 11:24.642
You can borrow my black heels if you want.

11:24.725 --> 11:26.560
The ones you wore to Cathy's birthday.

11:26.644 --> 11:28.938
I'm fine. Thanks.

11:37.113 --> 11:38.489
[whimpering]

11:55.339 --> 11:56.632
Ugh.

12:10.938 --> 12:13.232
Come on.
Time to go.

12:13.315 --> 12:14.817
[groans softly]

12:17.069 --> 12:18.946
- Come on.
- [sighs]

12:20.739 --> 12:23.909
[Pastor Charles] "Fear not,
for I am with you.

12:23.993 --> 12:26.662
Be not dismayed, for I am your God.

12:28.330 --> 12:29.623
I will strengthen you.

12:29.707 --> 12:31.876
Yes, I will help you.

12:31.959 --> 12:35.629
I will uphold you
with my righteous right hand."

12:37.006 --> 12:40.092
It's times like these
that our faith is challenged.

12:41.135 --> 12:43.721
How, if He is truly benevolent...

12:44.680 --> 12:48.058
could God take from us
someone so young, so innocent?

12:49.393 --> 12:51.979
It would be easy to turn away from God...

12:53.063 --> 12:55.316
but we must remember that nothing,

12:55.399 --> 12:58.027
not even tragedy,
can separate us from His love.

12:58.110 --> 13:01.113
We are here today to find comfort
in the truth of scripture,

13:01.197 --> 13:03.449
- and to surround Will and his family...
- [sniffles]

13:03.532 --> 13:06.869
Just wait till we tell Will that
Jennifer Hayes was crying at his funeral.

13:06.952 --> 13:08.245
[shushes]

13:17.004 --> 13:20.090
- [Karen] I'm so, so sorry.
- Oh, thank you so much for coming.

13:20.174 --> 13:22.259
Yeah, if there's anything we can do...

13:22.343 --> 13:24.386
I appreciate it.
Thank you so much.

13:25.888 --> 13:27.640
Take care, all right?
Thanks.

13:28.682 --> 13:30.226
Thanks for coming out.

13:41.028 --> 13:43.280
I see Will the Wise is back.

13:45.157 --> 13:47.910
What's that shooting out of his cane?

13:47.993 --> 13:49.411
Fireballs.

13:49.495 --> 13:51.789
I couldn't find the red crayon,
so that's why it's green.

13:51.872 --> 13:52.915
Oh, well...

13:52.998 --> 13:56.043
if he's so wise,
why does he need fireballs?

13:56.126 --> 13:59.338
Why can't he just, you know,
outsmart the bad guys?

14:00.381 --> 14:02.049
Most of the time, yeah, totally.

14:02.132 --> 14:05.427
But... sometimes the bad guys
are smart, too.

14:05.511 --> 14:06.512
You know?

14:07.054 --> 14:08.055
Yeah.

14:08.138 --> 14:10.099
So he needs the fireballs?

14:10.182 --> 14:12.977
Well, yeah, to burn them to a crisp.

14:13.060 --> 14:15.479
All right. Well, I don't know
who's been raising you,

14:15.563 --> 14:17.982
but I'm gonna get you some new crayons

14:18.065 --> 14:20.985
because it looks like
he's shooting cabbages.

14:21.068 --> 14:22.319
[both chuckle]

14:26.198 --> 14:27.491
[gasping]

14:32.246 --> 14:33.956
[breathing heavily]

14:47.595 --> 14:49.096
[panting]

15:13.662 --> 15:15.497
[dishes clattering]

16:03.295 --> 16:04.630
[exclaims]

16:11.887 --> 16:13.764
[Lucas] Will! Will, it's us!
Are you there?

16:13.847 --> 16:15.224
[Dustin] Can you hear us?
We're here!

16:15.307 --> 16:17.601
[Will distorted] Hello? Mom?

16:17.685 --> 16:20.479
[Lucas] Why can't he hear us?
[Mike] I don't know!

16:20.604 --> 16:22.898
[Will distorted] Mom, it's coming!

16:24.274 --> 16:26.193
She was there.

16:26.276 --> 16:27.987
[church bell tolling]

16:28.070 --> 16:30.823
This is where we know
for sure it's been, right?

16:30.906 --> 16:33.367
- So, that's...
- Steve's house.

16:33.450 --> 16:35.995
And that's the woods
where they found Will's bike and...

16:36.078 --> 16:37.162
that's my house.

16:37.246 --> 16:40.124
- It's all so close.
- Yeah. Exactly.

16:40.207 --> 16:42.584
I mean, it's all within a mile
or something.

16:42.668 --> 16:45.963
Whatever this thing is, it's...
it's not traveling far.

16:47.297 --> 16:48.966
You want to go out there.

16:49.508 --> 16:50.843
We might not find anything.

16:50.926 --> 16:52.261
I found something.

16:55.597 --> 16:58.142
And if we do see it...

17:00.477 --> 17:02.229
then what?

17:02.354 --> 17:03.397
[sighs]

17:05.607 --> 17:07.067
We kill it.

17:09.570 --> 17:12.364
- What are you doing?
- Just give me a second.

17:21.040 --> 17:22.541
Are you serious?

17:22.624 --> 17:27.004
What? You want to find this thing
and take another photo? Yell at it?

17:29.548 --> 17:31.258
This is a terrible idea.

17:31.341 --> 17:33.886
Yeah, well, it's the best we've got.

17:33.969 --> 17:36.889
What? You can tell someone,
but they're not gonna believe you.

17:36.972 --> 17:37.973
You know that.

17:38.057 --> 17:39.308
Your mom would.

17:39.391 --> 17:41.894
- She's been through enough.
- She deserves to know.

17:41.977 --> 17:43.353
Yeah, and I'll tell her...

17:43.437 --> 17:44.813
when this thing is dead.

17:49.359 --> 17:51.445
I don't know what in the world caused it.

17:51.528 --> 17:53.697
- Let's take a look, huh?
- Yeah.

17:53.781 --> 17:56.450
Mr. Clarke says
he's never seen anything like it.

17:56.533 --> 17:57.576
Mr. Clarke?

17:58.494 --> 18:00.579
Yeah, he runs the AV Club.

18:00.662 --> 18:01.789
[repairman] That right?

18:01.872 --> 18:05.250
Apparently, some of the less
athletic types go nuts for this stuff.

18:11.048 --> 18:12.549
[engine starting]

18:21.850 --> 18:23.602
[indistinct chatter]

18:40.202 --> 18:41.870
[Mike] Mr. Clarke?

18:41.954 --> 18:43.580
Oh, hey, there.

18:44.414 --> 18:45.791
How are you boys holding up?

18:45.874 --> 18:47.501
[munching]

18:47.584 --> 18:50.003
We're... in... mourning.

18:50.087 --> 18:52.840
Man, these aren't real Nilla Wafers.

18:52.923 --> 18:55.217
[Mike] We were wondering
if you had time to talk?

18:55.300 --> 18:58.470
- We have some questions.
- A lot of questions.

18:58.554 --> 19:01.473
So, you know how in Cosmos,
Carl Sagan talks about other dimensions?

19:01.557 --> 19:02.683
Like, beyond our world?

19:02.766 --> 19:04.268
Yeah, sure. Theoretically.

19:04.351 --> 19:05.435
Right, theoretically.

19:05.519 --> 19:08.105
So, theoretically,
how do we travel there?

19:08.188 --> 19:09.398
You guys have been thinking about

19:09.481 --> 19:11.567
Hugh Everett's Many-Worlds Interpretation,
haven't you?

19:13.777 --> 19:16.280
Well, basically,
there are parallel universes.

19:16.363 --> 19:17.781
Just like our world,

19:17.865 --> 19:20.325
but just infinite variations of it.

19:21.285 --> 19:22.578
Which means there's a world out there

19:22.661 --> 19:24.246
where none of this tragic stuff
ever happened.

19:24.329 --> 19:26.498
Yeah, that's not what we're talking about.

19:26.582 --> 19:28.125
- Oh.
- We were thinking of more

19:28.208 --> 19:29.751
of an evil dimension,
like the Vale of Shadows.

19:29.835 --> 19:31.378
You know the Vale of Shadows?

19:31.461 --> 19:33.881
An echo of the Material Plane,
where necrotic and shadow magic--

19:33.964 --> 19:34.965
[Mike] Yeah, exactly.

19:35.048 --> 19:37.426
If that did exist,
a place like the Vale of Shadows,

19:37.509 --> 19:38.844
how would we travel there?

19:38.927 --> 19:40.804
- Theoretically.
- [Mr. Clarke] Well...

19:45.767 --> 19:48.353
Picture... an acrobat...

19:49.605 --> 19:50.814
standing on a tightrope.

19:50.898 --> 19:53.525
Now, the tightrope is our dimension.

19:53.609 --> 19:56.069
And our dimension has rules.

19:56.153 --> 19:58.989
You can move forwards, or backwards.

19:59.531 --> 20:01.074
But, what if...

20:01.158 --> 20:05.287
right next to our acrobat,
there is a flea?

20:05.370 --> 20:08.498
Now, the flea can also travel
back and forth, just like the acrobat.

20:08.582 --> 20:09.875
- Right?
- Right.

20:09.958 --> 20:12.252
Here's where things
get really interesting.

20:12.336 --> 20:14.630
The flea can also travel this way...

20:14.713 --> 20:16.840
along the side of the rope.

20:16.924 --> 20:18.550
He can even go...

20:19.509 --> 20:20.719
underneath the rope.

20:20.802 --> 20:23.013
- [boys] Upside down.
- Exactly.

20:23.096 --> 20:25.349
But we're not the flea,
we're the acrobat.

20:25.432 --> 20:27.684
In this metaphor, yes,
we're the acrobat.

20:27.768 --> 20:29.353
So we can't go upside down?

20:29.436 --> 20:30.771
No.

20:30.854 --> 20:33.607
Well, is there any way for the acrobat
to get to the Upside Down?

20:33.690 --> 20:37.277
Well... you'd have to create
a massive amount of energy.

20:37.361 --> 20:40.155
More than humans are currently
capable of creating, mind you,

20:40.239 --> 20:42.532
to open up some kind of tear
in time and space,

20:42.616 --> 20:43.825
and then...

20:49.873 --> 20:51.083
you create a doorway.

20:51.166 --> 20:53.835
- Like a gate?
- Sure. Like a gate.

20:53.919 --> 20:56.713
- But again, this is all--
- Theoretical.

20:56.797 --> 20:59.258
But... but what if this gate
already existed?

20:59.341 --> 21:01.760
Well, if it did, I... I think we'd know.

21:01.843 --> 21:03.136
It would disrupt gravity,

21:03.220 --> 21:05.180
the magnetic field, our environment.

21:05.264 --> 21:07.683
Heck, it might even swallow us up whole.

21:09.142 --> 21:10.477
Science is neat.

21:10.560 --> 21:13.313
But I'm afraid it's not very forgiving.

21:24.825 --> 21:25.951
Whoa.

21:29.454 --> 21:30.539
Hey, Chief!

21:31.456 --> 21:33.250
- [knocking] Hello? Whoa!
- Hey!

21:34.918 --> 21:37.546
- [Powell] Jesus, Chief. You all right?
- What are you doing here?

21:37.629 --> 21:41.300
- We tried calling, but--
- Yeah, the phone's dead.

21:42.884 --> 21:45.804
Hey, so Bev Mooney came in
this morning all upset.

21:45.887 --> 21:48.557
Said that Dale and Henry
went hunting yesterday...

21:48.640 --> 21:49.683
and they didn't come back home.

21:49.766 --> 21:54.229
She thought they were on another binger,
but she's not so sure now.

21:55.439 --> 21:58.567
I think this whole Will Byers thing
has everybody on edge.

21:58.650 --> 22:00.277
- Where was this?
- It was at the station.

22:00.360 --> 22:02.279
No, no. Where did Henry
and Dale go hunting?

22:02.362 --> 22:04.239
Oh. Uh, out near Kerley.

22:04.740 --> 22:05.824
Mirkwood.

22:05.907 --> 22:07.159
What?

22:07.659 --> 22:08.994
Okay.

22:09.077 --> 22:11.788
You go back to the station.
I'll take care of this, all right?

22:12.331 --> 22:14.541
- Are you sure?
- Yeah, leave it.

22:14.624 --> 22:18.086
- Oh, hey. Uh, they found Barbara's car.
- What?

22:18.170 --> 22:21.089
Barbara Holland's car.
Seems she ran away after all.

22:21.173 --> 22:23.717
Staties found it late last night
at a bus station.

22:23.800 --> 22:26.845
Funny, right?
They keep doing our job for us.

22:28.597 --> 22:30.724
Yeah.
[chuckles] It's funny.

22:32.684 --> 22:33.894
[door closes]

22:35.562 --> 22:37.856
- Is he off his meds again?
- [engine starts]

22:37.939 --> 22:40.567
He's been spending too much time
with Joyce Byers.

22:40.650 --> 22:41.902
That's what I think.

22:48.992 --> 22:51.036
[sighs] What are you doing?

22:51.119 --> 22:52.746
What does it look like I'm doing?

22:52.829 --> 22:54.372
You want to freeze to death all winter?

22:55.123 --> 22:56.374
[hammering continues]

22:57.334 --> 23:01.588
- I told you not to take these down.
- They were in the way, babe.

23:01.671 --> 23:04.049
How long are you gonna keep those up?
I mean, really?

23:08.845 --> 23:11.389
You know, it's a shame
what they've done to this family.

23:11.473 --> 23:13.266
- What?
- The Sattler Company.

23:13.350 --> 23:15.060
I went to the quarry
on the way over here.

23:15.143 --> 23:17.145
I just wanted to look around, you know?

23:17.229 --> 23:19.731
Couldn't believe it.
I just couldn't believe it.

23:21.858 --> 23:24.402
No warning signs, no fence, no nothing.

23:25.362 --> 23:27.531
Ought to be held accountable
if you ask me.

23:31.910 --> 23:34.412
It would take a lot of energy
to build a gate like this.

23:34.496 --> 23:35.580
But that's got to be what happened.

23:35.664 --> 23:39.709
- Otherwise, how'd Will get there, right?
- Right.

23:39.793 --> 23:42.671
What we want to know is,
do you know where the gate is?

23:44.631 --> 23:46.508
Then how do you know
about the Upside Down?

23:53.390 --> 23:55.559
[Mike] Dustin, what are you doing?
Dustin?

23:55.642 --> 23:56.893
- Dustin!
- Dustin!

23:57.936 --> 23:59.896
- I... I need to see your compasses.
- What?

23:59.980 --> 24:02.274
Your compasses.
All of your compasses, right now!

24:07.779 --> 24:09.364
What's exciting about this?

24:09.447 --> 24:12.951
- Well, they're all facing north, right?
- Yeah, so?

24:13.034 --> 24:14.911
- Well, that's not true north.
- What do you mean?

24:14.995 --> 24:18.081
I mean exactly what I just said.
That's not true north.

24:18.165 --> 24:20.500
Are you both seriously this dense?

24:22.210 --> 24:26.715
The sun rises in the east,
and it sets in the west. Right?

24:26.798 --> 24:29.176
Which means that's true north.

24:29.259 --> 24:30.969
So what you're saying
is the compasses are broken.

24:31.052 --> 24:34.514
[sighs] Do you even understand
how a compass works?

24:34.598 --> 24:36.850
- Do you see a battery pack on this?
- No.

24:36.933 --> 24:38.935
No, you don't.
Because it doesn't need one.

24:39.019 --> 24:41.813
The needle's naturally drawn
to the Earth's magnetic North Pole.

24:41.897 --> 24:43.315
So what's wrong with them?

24:43.398 --> 24:45.400
Well, that's what I couldn't figure out,
but then I remembered.

24:45.483 --> 24:47.527
You can change the direction
of a compass with a magnet.

24:47.611 --> 24:49.988
If there's the presence
of a more powerful magnetic field,

24:50.071 --> 24:51.781
the needle deflects to that power.

24:51.865 --> 24:53.533
And then I remembered
what Mr. Clarke said.

24:53.617 --> 24:55.285
The gate would have so much power--

24:55.368 --> 24:56.953
It could disrupt
the electromagnetic field.

24:57.037 --> 24:58.079
Exactly.

24:58.163 --> 25:00.540
Meaning, if we follow
the compasses' north...

25:00.624 --> 25:02.584
They should lead us to the gate.

25:32.489 --> 25:33.782
[sighs]

25:56.805 --> 25:58.139
Whoa, whoa, hey, whoa, whoa.

25:58.223 --> 26:01.476
- [exhales] What are you doing here?
- What are you doing?

26:02.185 --> 26:05.146
- Nothing.
- I hope that's not meant for me.

26:05.230 --> 26:06.606
What? No.

26:06.690 --> 26:08.733
Oh, no, I was just...

26:09.693 --> 26:12.570
- thinking about joining softball.
- Oh.

26:13.530 --> 26:14.531
Well, uh...

26:14.614 --> 26:15.949
listen, I'm really sorry.

26:16.032 --> 26:18.785
I mean, even before you threatened me
with the baseball bat.

26:20.245 --> 26:21.621
Okay.

26:21.705 --> 26:23.206
I panicked and...

26:23.290 --> 26:24.374
[sighs]

26:24.457 --> 26:28.086
- I mean, I was a total dick.
- Yeah, you were.

26:28.169 --> 26:29.587
[sighs]

26:31.631 --> 26:33.800
Did you get in trouble with your parents?

26:33.883 --> 26:35.135
Totally, but...

26:35.218 --> 26:37.387
you know, who cares?
Screw 'em.

26:37.470 --> 26:39.306
Any news about Barbara?

26:41.141 --> 26:43.351
- Parents heard from her? Or...
- No.

26:46.521 --> 26:47.522
Hey, listen.

26:47.605 --> 26:50.608
Why don't we, uh, why don't we
catch a movie tonight, you know?

26:50.692 --> 26:53.361
Just kinda pretend everything's normal
for a few hours.

26:53.445 --> 26:55.238
All The Right Moves is still playing.

26:56.156 --> 26:58.742
You know, with your lover boy
from Risky Business? [chuckles]

26:58.825 --> 26:59.868
Yeah, I know.

26:59.951 --> 27:01.619
You know, Carol thinks
I actually kinda look like him.

27:01.703 --> 27:03.455
What do you think? Huh?

27:03.538 --> 27:06.624
♪ Just take those old records
Off the shelf ♪

27:06.708 --> 27:09.210
♪ I'll sit and listen to them by myself ♪

27:09.294 --> 27:12.672
I just, I... I don't think I can.

27:13.673 --> 27:17.218
I've been really busy
with this whole funeral thing and...

27:17.302 --> 27:19.429
[inhales] with my brother.

27:19.512 --> 27:21.222
It's been really hard on him.

27:21.306 --> 27:23.933
Yeah, sure.
Sure, yeah, yeah.

27:25.852 --> 27:27.395
So...

27:30.023 --> 27:31.149
I should go.

27:32.150 --> 27:33.318
Sorry.

27:35.278 --> 27:38.031
I'll call you later.
Is that okay?

27:40.617 --> 27:42.786
Yeah.
Yeah, of course.

27:44.662 --> 27:47.749
♪ Just take those old records
Off the shelf ♪

27:47.832 --> 27:49.417
♪ I'll sit and listen to them... ♪

27:49.501 --> 27:50.543
[sighs]

28:01.096 --> 28:02.889
[Joyce] You were here for the money!
[Lonnie] No!

28:02.972 --> 28:05.558
The money!
Admit it!

28:05.642 --> 28:09.187
You aren't here 'cause of Will.
You never cared about him. You never did!

28:09.312 --> 28:12.565
Jesus, Joyce, it was his funeral today.
Do we have to do this right now?

28:12.649 --> 28:15.193
- I can't believe I fell for this.
- I'm here to help, Joyce.

28:15.276 --> 28:17.529
- To help?
- We could use that money for good.

28:17.612 --> 28:20.281
[stammering] Oh, like maybe
to pay off your debts?

28:20.365 --> 28:22.700
To pay for Jonathan to go to school!

28:22.784 --> 28:24.911
- Oh, don't do that.
- Do what?

28:24.994 --> 28:26.996
- Lie to me!
- I'm not lying to you!

28:27.080 --> 28:28.456
Yeah, well, where does he wanna go?

28:28.540 --> 28:29.582
- Huh?
- What?

28:29.666 --> 28:31.251
Where does Jonathan
want to go to college?

28:31.334 --> 28:33.253
We get that money,
anywhere he damn well pleases!

28:33.336 --> 28:35.088
NYU, Lonnie!

28:35.171 --> 28:38.717
He's wanted to go to NYU
since he was six years old!

28:38.800 --> 28:41.094
So then he goes to NYU!

28:41.177 --> 28:42.345
Get out.

28:43.471 --> 28:44.889
Get out!

28:45.724 --> 28:46.975
You need me here, Joyce.

28:47.058 --> 28:50.687
[laughs] Oh, brother,
I have not needed you for a long time!

28:50.770 --> 28:53.064
Oh, no?
Look what happened.

28:53.773 --> 28:54.899
[scoffs]

28:54.983 --> 28:57.318
Oh, don't you dare.

28:57.402 --> 28:59.863
- At least I was here!
- Oh, come on, Joyce.

28:59.946 --> 29:01.823
Just look around at this place.

29:01.906 --> 29:03.283
All your Christmas lights.

29:03.366 --> 29:06.453
What the hell am I supposed to think?
You're such a great mom? You're a mess!

29:06.536 --> 29:09.748
Maybe I am a mess. Maybe I'm crazy.
Maybe I'm out of my mind!

29:09.831 --> 29:14.836
But, God help me, I will keep
these lights up until the day I die

29:14.919 --> 29:17.630
if I think there's a chance
that Will's still out there!

29:19.174 --> 29:21.634
Now, get out!

29:22.218 --> 29:23.803
Get out of my house!

29:33.229 --> 29:34.272
[sighs]

29:36.316 --> 29:38.943
[Nancy] You're supposed
to hit the cans, right?

29:40.528 --> 29:44.157
No, actually, you see the spaces
in between the cans?

29:44.240 --> 29:46.117
I'm aiming for those.

29:46.201 --> 29:47.243
Ah.

29:49.496 --> 29:51.080
You ever shot a gun before?

29:51.164 --> 29:54.042
- [scoffs] Have you met my parents?
- [chuckles softly]

29:54.959 --> 29:57.003
Yeah, I haven't shot one since I was ten.

29:57.879 --> 30:00.507
My dad took me hunting on my birthday.

30:00.590 --> 30:02.342
He made me kill a rabbit.

30:02.425 --> 30:04.219
- A rabbit?
- Yeah.

30:04.302 --> 30:08.014
I guess he thought it would make me
into more of a man or something.

30:09.140 --> 30:10.433
I cried for a week.

30:10.517 --> 30:11.684
Jesus.

30:11.768 --> 30:14.020
What? I'm a fan of Thumper.

30:14.103 --> 30:17.398
[chuckles] I meant your dad.

30:17.482 --> 30:18.691
Yeah.

30:19.901 --> 30:22.737
I guess he and my mother
loved each other at some point, but...

30:22.821 --> 30:24.405
[gun cocks]

30:24.489 --> 30:26.157
...I wasn't around for that part.

30:28.535 --> 30:29.702
Um, yeah.

30:30.745 --> 30:33.248
Just, uh, point and shoot.

30:35.792 --> 30:38.211
I don't think my parents
ever loved each other.

30:38.294 --> 30:40.255
They must've married for some reason.

30:40.338 --> 30:41.714
My mom was young.

30:42.674 --> 30:44.175
My dad was older,

30:44.259 --> 30:49.347
but he had a cushy job, money,
came from a good family.

30:50.974 --> 30:54.727
So they bought a nice house
at the end of the cul-de-sac...

30:54.811 --> 30:56.771
and started their nuclear family.

30:57.856 --> 30:59.691
Screw that.

30:59.774 --> 31:00.942
Yeah.

31:02.318 --> 31:03.570
Screw that.

31:06.072 --> 31:08.533
- [beer can clatters]
- [both chuckle]

31:09.284 --> 31:10.785
[dialing]

31:12.120 --> 31:13.621
[line ringing]

31:16.165 --> 31:18.084
- [woman] Hello?
- Hey.

31:19.085 --> 31:20.670
Jim?

31:20.753 --> 31:22.505
- Yeah.
- [sighs]

31:22.589 --> 31:25.258
Why are you calling me here?
I told you not to call me.

31:25.341 --> 31:27.969
I know, I know, I know.
I just wanted to...

31:28.052 --> 31:30.430
I just wanted to hear your voice
and, uh...

31:32.390 --> 31:34.642
I just wanted to say that, um...

31:39.105 --> 31:41.524
even after everything that happened,
I don't...

31:42.859 --> 31:44.277
I don't regret any of it.

31:44.360 --> 31:47.238
And those seven years, they were...
everything to me.

31:48.197 --> 31:49.782
Have you been drinking?

31:51.326 --> 31:52.577
No.

31:53.661 --> 31:55.830
- No.
- [baby crying over phone]

31:55.914 --> 31:58.041
[woman shushing] Honey, hey...

31:58.124 --> 32:01.210
Hey, it's okay...
it's okay. [shushing]

32:01.294 --> 32:02.545
[crying continues]

32:02.629 --> 32:04.923
You know what, actually,
I have been drinking, I'm sorry.

32:05.006 --> 32:06.382
Jim, I can't...

32:06.466 --> 32:08.551
Just take care of yourself, okay?

32:08.635 --> 32:10.011
Say hi to Bill for me.

32:10.094 --> 32:11.387
Are you sure--

32:22.899 --> 32:25.151
[phone ringing]

32:29.322 --> 32:30.531
[ringing stops]

32:38.039 --> 32:40.249
[sighs] How much further?

32:41.292 --> 32:44.420
I don't know.
These only tell direction, not distance.

32:45.505 --> 32:47.715
You really need to learn more
about compasses.

32:47.799 --> 32:49.258
I'm just saying.

32:49.342 --> 32:51.761
How do we know
when we get to the gate?

32:51.844 --> 32:55.515
Uh, I think a portal to another dimension
is gonna be pretty obvious.

32:56.516 --> 32:57.725
[sighs]

33:00.478 --> 33:02.021
Do you think she's acting weird?

33:03.231 --> 33:05.692
You're asking if the weirdo
is acting weird?

33:07.026 --> 33:08.903
I mean, weirder than normal?

33:10.029 --> 33:11.614
I don't know.
Who cares?

33:14.701 --> 33:16.619
[breathing heavily]

33:20.748 --> 33:21.916
How far, Papa?

33:22.000 --> 33:24.252
Farther than we've ever gone before.

33:29.007 --> 33:30.633
- The bath?
- Yes.

33:31.676 --> 33:33.261
Yes, the bath.

33:36.681 --> 33:38.057
Is that okay?

33:42.311 --> 33:43.354
Okay.

35:12.527 --> 35:13.569
Mike.

35:14.529 --> 35:16.030
Yeah?

35:16.114 --> 35:17.657
Turn back.

35:17.740 --> 35:19.283
What? Why?

35:19.367 --> 35:20.451
I'm tired.

35:20.535 --> 35:24.330
[sighs] Look, I'm sure we're almost there.
Just hold on a little longer, okay?

35:32.547 --> 35:33.589
[sighs]

35:42.348 --> 35:44.142
[Nancy] You never said what I was saying.

35:45.393 --> 35:46.602
[Jonathan] What?

35:46.686 --> 35:47.812
[Nancy] Yesterday.

35:47.895 --> 35:51.440
You said I was saying something
and that's why you took my picture.

35:51.524 --> 35:52.692
Oh, uh...

35:53.609 --> 35:54.861
I don't know.

35:57.280 --> 35:58.531
My guess...

36:00.533 --> 36:04.745
I saw this girl, you know,
trying to be someone else.

36:04.829 --> 36:07.290
But for that moment...

36:07.373 --> 36:09.834
it was like you were alone,
or you thought you were.

36:09.917 --> 36:12.295
And, you know,
you could just be yourself.

36:15.214 --> 36:18.092
That is such bullshit.

36:18.176 --> 36:19.552
[stammers] What?

36:20.970 --> 36:23.264
I am not trying to be someone else.

36:23.347 --> 36:25.850
Just because I'm dating Steve
and you don't like him--

36:25.933 --> 36:28.561
You know what? Forget it.
I just thought it was a good picture.

36:28.644 --> 36:30.646
He's actually a good guy.

36:30.730 --> 36:31.814
[stammers] Okay.

36:32.857 --> 36:35.193
Yesterday, with the camera...

36:36.194 --> 36:38.321
He's not like that at all.

36:38.404 --> 36:40.698
He was just being protective.

36:40.781 --> 36:42.617
Yeah, that's one word for it.

36:43.576 --> 36:45.536
Oh, and I guess what you did was okay?

36:45.620 --> 36:47.121
No, I... I never said that.

36:47.205 --> 36:48.414
He had every right to be pissed--

36:48.497 --> 36:50.541
Okay, all right.
Does that mean I have to like him?

36:50.625 --> 36:52.043
No.

36:52.126 --> 36:54.212
Listen, don't take it so personally, okay?

36:54.295 --> 36:57.173
I don't like most people.
He's in the vast majority.

36:59.133 --> 37:02.261
You know, I was actually starting
to think that you were okay.

37:02.345 --> 37:04.180
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

37:04.263 --> 37:07.350
Yeah, I was thinking,
"Jonathan Byers,

37:07.433 --> 37:10.645
maybe he's not the pretentious creep
everyone says he is."

37:10.728 --> 37:12.730
Well, I was just starting
to think you were okay.

37:12.813 --> 37:14.690
- Oh.
- I was thinking, "Nancy Wheeler,

37:14.774 --> 37:17.693
she's not just another suburban girl
who thinks she's rebelling

37:17.777 --> 37:20.696
by doing exactly what
every other suburban girl does...

37:20.780 --> 37:23.407
until that phase passes
and they marry some boring one-time jock

37:23.491 --> 37:24.659
who now works sales,

37:24.742 --> 37:27.578
and they live out a perfectly boring
little life at the end of a cul-de-sac.

37:27.662 --> 37:30.998
Exactly like their parents,
who they thought were so depressing,

37:31.082 --> 37:33.084
but now, hey, they get it."

37:33.626 --> 37:34.669
[scoffs]

37:39.632 --> 37:40.633
[scoffs]

37:41.425 --> 37:42.510
[pounding on door]

37:43.594 --> 37:45.304
Go away, Lonnie.

37:45.388 --> 37:46.555
[continues pounding]

37:48.266 --> 37:49.308
Seriously!

37:50.226 --> 37:52.228
I am gonna murd--

37:54.605 --> 37:57.108
[whispers] What? What?

38:04.949 --> 38:06.242
[Hopper sighs]

38:07.952 --> 38:09.287
Oh, Jesus.

38:10.288 --> 38:11.914
[scoffs]

38:23.175 --> 38:24.802
Oh, no.

38:24.885 --> 38:27.680
"Oh, no"?
What's, "Oh, no"?

38:27.763 --> 38:29.223
- We're headed back home.
- What?

38:29.307 --> 38:30.558
- Are you sure?
- Yeah, I'm sure.

38:30.641 --> 38:32.977
Setting sun, right there.
We looped right back around.

38:33.060 --> 38:35.604
And you're just realizing this now?

38:35.688 --> 38:38.607
- Why is this all on me?
- Because you're the compass genius!

38:38.691 --> 38:40.192
What do yours say?

38:40.276 --> 38:41.819
[both] North.

38:41.902 --> 38:44.363
- [Lucas sighs]
- Makes no damn sense.

38:44.447 --> 38:46.407
[sighs] Maybe the gate moved.

38:46.490 --> 38:48.367
No, I don't think it's the gate.

38:48.451 --> 38:51.287
I think it's something else
screwing with the compasses.

38:51.370 --> 38:53.122
Maybe it's something here.

38:53.205 --> 38:55.624
No, it has to be like a super magnet.

38:55.708 --> 38:57.376
It's not a magnet.

38:57.460 --> 38:59.378
She's been acting weirder than normal.

38:59.462 --> 39:02.298
If she can slam doors with her mind,
she can definitely screw up a compass.

39:02.381 --> 39:03.424
Why would she do that?

39:03.507 --> 39:05.468
Because she's trying
to sabotage our mission.

39:05.551 --> 39:06.886
Because she's a traitor!

39:09.013 --> 39:10.931
[Mike] Lucas, what are you doing?

39:12.600 --> 39:14.852
You did it, didn't you?

39:14.935 --> 39:16.729
You don't want us to reach the gate.

39:16.812 --> 39:18.647
You don't want us to find Will.

39:18.731 --> 39:19.857
[Mike] Lucas, come on, seriously,

39:19.940 --> 39:21.108
- just leave her alone!
- Admit it.

39:21.192 --> 39:22.818
- No.
- Admit it!

39:23.903 --> 39:26.989
- Fresh blood. I knew it.
- Lucas, come on!

39:27.073 --> 39:30.159
I saw her wiping her nose on the tracks!
She was using her powers!

39:30.242 --> 39:32.036
Bull! That's old blood.
Right, El?

39:33.579 --> 39:35.206
Right, El?

39:37.958 --> 39:39.877
[crying] It's... not...

39:41.462 --> 39:43.506
it's not safe.

39:50.221 --> 39:52.598
- [Hopper panting]
- [light bulbs clattering]

40:03.442 --> 40:05.069
[panting] Okay.

40:05.152 --> 40:06.737
Should be okay, I mean...

40:07.696 --> 40:09.824
I can't guarantee it,
but it should be okay.

40:09.907 --> 40:11.575
What the hell is going on, Hopper?

40:11.659 --> 40:13.869
They bugged my place.

40:13.953 --> 40:15.287
- What?
- They bugged my place.

40:15.371 --> 40:17.832
They put a microphone in the light.

40:18.999 --> 40:20.459
[sighs]

40:20.543 --> 40:23.170
It's because I'm on to them
and they know it.

40:23.254 --> 40:24.964
- I don't know...
- Who?

40:25.047 --> 40:26.424
I thought they might be watching you, too.

40:26.507 --> 40:29.760
I don't know, the CIA, the NSA,
Department of Energy... I don't know.

40:29.844 --> 40:32.012
You gotta explain this to me,
'cause I am not--

40:32.096 --> 40:35.141
I went to the morgue last night, Joyce.

40:36.559 --> 40:37.601
What?

40:38.227 --> 40:39.603
It wasn't him.

40:40.479 --> 40:41.522
What?

40:41.605 --> 40:43.732
Will's body, it was a fake.

40:46.861 --> 40:48.320
[Joyce exclaims]

40:50.948 --> 40:52.408
You were right.

40:55.077 --> 40:57.079
This whole time, you were right.

41:02.585 --> 41:05.212
What did I tell you?
She's been playing us from the beginning!

41:05.296 --> 41:07.506
That's not true.
She helped us find Will!

41:07.590 --> 41:08.591
Find Will?

41:08.674 --> 41:09.925
Find Will?

41:10.009 --> 41:13.387
Where is he, then? Huh?
I don't see him.

41:13.471 --> 41:16.140
- Yeah, you know what I mean.
- No, I actually don't.

41:16.223 --> 41:17.475
Just think about it, Mike.

41:17.558 --> 41:20.227
She could've just told us where
the Upside Down was right away,

41:20.311 --> 41:21.395
but she didn't.

41:21.479 --> 41:23.439
She just made us
run around like headless chickens.

41:23.522 --> 41:25.149
- All right, calm down!
- No!

41:25.232 --> 41:27.026
She used us, all of us!

41:27.109 --> 41:29.487
She helped just enough
so she could get what she wants.

41:29.570 --> 41:30.779
Food and a bed.

41:30.863 --> 41:33.073
- She's like a stray dog.
- Screw you, Lucas!

41:33.157 --> 41:35.159
No! Screw you, Mike.
You're blind...

41:35.242 --> 41:37.536
blind because you like
that a girl's not grossed out by you.

41:37.620 --> 41:40.080
But wake up, man!
Wake the hell up!

41:40.998 --> 41:42.208
She knows where Will is,

41:42.291 --> 41:45.085
and now she's just letting him die
in the Upside Down.

41:45.169 --> 41:47.630
- Shut up!
- For all we know, it's her fault.

41:47.713 --> 41:48.756
Shut up.

41:48.839 --> 41:51.300
We're looking for some stupid monster...

41:51.383 --> 41:55.596
but did you ever stop to think
that maybe she's the monster?

41:56.722 --> 41:58.015
I said shut up!

41:58.098 --> 41:59.266
[grunting]

41:59.350 --> 42:01.519
- Stop!
- [Dustin] Knock it off, you idiots.

42:01.602 --> 42:02.937
- Stop it!
- [Dustin] Mike, get off!

42:03.020 --> 42:04.980
- Stop it!
- [grunting]

42:05.064 --> 42:06.398
[screams]

42:10.444 --> 42:11.445
Jesus!

42:13.697 --> 42:16.325
Lucas! Lucas!
Lucas, are you all right?

42:16.408 --> 42:18.160
- Lucas.
- Lucas, come on!

42:18.244 --> 42:20.329
- Lucas, wake up! Lucas.
- [Dustin] Come on, Lucas!

42:20.412 --> 42:22.081
Why would you do that?

42:23.165 --> 42:24.542
- [Dustin] Come on.
- What's wrong with you?

42:25.459 --> 42:27.836
[distorted] What is wrong with you?

42:30.631 --> 42:32.883
[indistinct overlapping chatter]

42:35.844 --> 42:38.180
[chatter continues]

42:43.269 --> 42:45.771
[man speaking Russian]

43:03.372 --> 43:05.332
[man continues speaking]

43:11.046 --> 43:13.632
[man continues indistinctly on PA]

43:20.848 --> 43:22.975
[speaking Russian]

43:33.319 --> 43:34.570
[growling]

43:40.159 --> 43:42.995
- [distorted snarling on PA]
- What is that?

43:43.996 --> 43:45.414
I have no idea.

43:47.082 --> 43:49.460
[breathing heavily]

43:55.507 --> 43:57.635
[indistinct snarling]

44:00.804 --> 44:04.308
[muffled screaming] Help! Help!

44:05.184 --> 44:06.810
[sobbing]

44:06.894 --> 44:08.187
[Dustin] Come on, wake up.
Come on!

44:09.396 --> 44:11.231
Lucas...
Lucas, come on!

44:12.608 --> 44:13.901
- [Dustin chuckles]
- [Mike exhales]

44:15.110 --> 44:16.403
[Mike] Lucas.

44:17.279 --> 44:18.572
Lucas, you okay?

44:19.823 --> 44:22.743
[Dustin] Lucas... Lucas,
how many fingers am I holding up?

44:22.826 --> 44:24.203
Lucas, how many fingers?

44:24.286 --> 44:26.288
- [Mike] Let me see your head.
- Get off of me!

44:26.372 --> 44:28.791
[Mike] Just... Lucas.
Lucas, let me see.

44:28.874 --> 44:30.167
Get off of me!

44:32.544 --> 44:34.296
- Lucas, come on.
- Let him go.

44:37.174 --> 44:38.592
Man, let him go.

44:43.222 --> 44:44.932
Where is El?

44:45.015 --> 44:46.850
[breathing heavily]

44:46.934 --> 44:48.143
[shouting] El?

44:48.977 --> 44:50.562
- El!
- Eleven!

44:52.439 --> 44:53.482
El!

44:54.858 --> 44:56.568
- Eleven!
- El!

44:57.736 --> 44:58.862
Eleven!

45:17.548 --> 45:19.299
[indistinct whimper]

45:23.971 --> 45:25.222
What, are you tired?

45:25.305 --> 45:26.765
Shut up.

45:26.849 --> 45:27.891
What?

45:30.227 --> 45:33.021
I heard something.

45:33.105 --> 45:34.356
[indistinct whimper]

45:36.775 --> 45:38.402
[whimpering continues]

45:52.875 --> 45:55.377
- [Nancy] Oh, God.
- [whimpering]

45:59.047 --> 46:00.632
[Jonathan sighs]

46:03.177 --> 46:04.845
[Nancy] It's been hit by a car.

46:11.101 --> 46:12.519
We can't just leave it.

46:24.531 --> 46:26.492
[whimpering]

46:28.243 --> 46:29.620
I'll do it.

46:31.705 --> 46:34.166
- I thought you said--
- I'm not nine anymore.

46:40.839 --> 46:42.257
[sighs]

46:43.342 --> 46:45.594
- [cocks gun]
- [breathing heavily]

46:58.440 --> 47:00.067
[both gasping]

47:03.237 --> 47:04.404
What was that?

47:04.488 --> 47:05.656
[both panting]

47:35.978 --> 47:37.312
[Nancy] Where'd it go?

47:38.939 --> 47:40.482
I don't know.

47:42.901 --> 47:44.528
Do you see any more blood?

47:45.571 --> 47:47.072
No.

48:04.590 --> 48:06.216
[liquid dripping]

48:33.702 --> 48:34.911
Jonathan?

49:48.735 --> 49:50.779
- [snarling]
- [gasps]

49:51.947 --> 49:53.407
[gnawing]

50:06.503 --> 50:08.296
- [twig snaps]
- [roars]

50:08.380 --> 50:09.756
[screams]

50:09.840 --> 50:11.383
[gasps] Nancy!

50:17.013 --> 50:18.432
Nancy?

50:19.766 --> 50:21.059
Nancy?

50:22.102 --> 50:23.687
Nancy, where are you?

50:26.106 --> 50:27.441
Nancy!

50:28.775 --> 50:29.985
Nancy!

50:36.575 --> 50:38.618
[rock music playing]
